
Exercise 1: Anatomy of the Ideal Prompt 
Question: Design a prompt that asks an LLM to create a three-section 
newsletter (intro, main content, conclusion) for a university tech club. 
Prompt: 
“You are an experienced newsletter writer for the ANITS Tech Club. 
• Task: Write a newsletter with three sections: Introduction, Main Content, 
and Conclusion. 
• Context: For ANITS students interested in technology events, coding 
workshops, and innovation updates. 
• Tone: Engaging, friendly, motivating. 
• Format: Use headings for each section. Keep each section within 150 
words. 
• Constraints: Focus only on ANITS activities and opportunities. 
• Example: ‘Welcome back, ANITS techies! This month we bring you 
exciting updates from our campus innovation circle…’” 
Exercise 2: What Kind of Document? 
Question: Write a prompt that makes the LLM generate a formal report (with 
headings) comparing electric cars and hybrid cars. 
Prompt: 
“You are a research analyst preparing a formal comparative report on electric 
vs hybrid cars. 
• Task: Compare electric and hybrid cars. 
• Context: Report for engineering students interested in sustainable 
transportation. 
• Persona: Professional automotive researcher. 
• Tone: Formal, factual, academic. 
• Format: Sections → Introduction, Advantages of Electric Cars, 
Advantages of Hybrid Cars, Key Differences, Conclusion. 
• Constraints: Limit to 500 words, objective only, no personal opinions.” 
Exercise 3: The Advice Conversation 
Question: Build a prompt where the model acts like a career counselor, giving 
advice to a student unsure whether to pursue higher studies or start working. 
Prompt: 
“You are a career counselor. 
• Task: Advise a student choosing between higher studies and a job. 
• Context: Recently completed undergraduate engineering. 
• Persona: Supportive advisor. 
• Tone: Empathetic and practical. 
• Format: Sections → Higher Studies Path, Working Path, Balanced 
Recommendation. 
• Constraints: Pros & cons for each, under 400 words, realistic advice.” 
Exercise 4: The Analytic Report 
Question: Create a prompt that produces an analytic report on the impact of 
smartphones on student productivity, with sections for findings and 
recommendations. 
Prompt: 
“You are an education researcher. 
• Task: Report on smartphones’ impact on student productivity. 
• Context: Audience = university faculty and students. 
• Persona: Neutral researcher. 
• Tone: Objective, data-driven. 
• Format: Sections → Findings, Recommendations. 
• Constraints: Use bullet points where needed, ≤500 words.” 
Exercise 5: The Structured Document 
Question: Write a prompt that gets the LLM to output a JSON list of three 
Indian festivals, with name, state, and month as fields. 
Prompt: 
“You are a data formatter. 
• Task: Output JSON list of 3 Indian festivals. 
• Format: JSON only, no extra text. 
• Constraints: Exactly 3 items, with fields → name, state, month. 
• Example: 
[ 
{ "name": "Diwali", "state": "All India", "month": "October-November" } 
] 
```”   
Exercise 6: Formatting Snippets 
Question: You are given this snippet: “The Taj Mahal attracts over 6 million 
visitors annually.” Create a prompt that uses this snippet while asking the 
model to compare it with other world monuments. 
Prompt: 
“You are a cultural heritage analyst. 
• Task: Compare the Taj Mahal with at least 2 other world monuments. 
• Context: Snippet → ‘The Taj Mahal attracts over 6 million visitors 
annually.’ 
• Format: Sections → Taj Mahal, Other Monuments, Comparative Analysis, 
Conclusion. 
• Constraints: Snippet must appear exactly. ≤400 words.” 
Exercise 7: More on Inertness 
Question: Suppose you have background text: “Some sources claim video 
games always harm students’ focus.” Write a prompt that makes the model 
discuss both pros and cons fairly. 
Prompt: 
“You are an education analyst. 
• Task: Discuss pros & cons of video games on student focus. 
• Context: Background text → ‘Some sources claim video games always 
harm students’ focus.’ 
• Format: Sections → Pros, Cons, Conclusion. 
• Constraints: Balanced view, ≤450 words.” 
Exercise 8: Formatting Few-Shot Examples 
Question: Provide two example translations from English to Hindi. Then, design 
a prompt so the LLM translates a third English phrase into Hindi in the same 
style. 
Prompt: 
“You are a translator. 
• Task: Translate English → Hindi in same style as examples. 
• Format: English: … | Hindi: … 
• Constraints: No transliterations or extra explanation. 
• Examples: 
o English: Good morning → Hindi: शुभ प्रभात 
o English: How are you? → Hindi: आप कैसे हैं? 
• New Phrase: English: Thank you” 
Exercise 9: Elastic Snippets 
Question: You have a long passage about renewable energy. Create a prompt 
that makes the LLM compress it into 3 concise bullet points. 
Prompt: 
“You are a content summarizer. 
• Task: Summarize renewable energy passage into 3 bullets. 
• Constraints: Exactly 3 points, ≤20 words each. No jargon. 
• Example: 
o Renewable energy reduces carbon emissions. 
o Solar and wind power expanding. 
o Storage challenges remain.” 
Exercise 10: Relationships Among Prompt Elements 
Question: Imagine you want the LLM to create a tagline for a startup app that 
delivers groceries. Write a prompt that clearly separates context, instruction, 
and constraints. 
Prompt: 
“You are a creative copywriter. 
• Task: Write a tagline for grocery delivery app. 
• Context: App delivers groceries in 30 minutes. 
• Tone: Energetic, catchy. 
• Constraints: Under 6 words. No clichés like ‘Fast and Fresh.’ 
• Example: ‘Groceries in a Flash’” 
Exercise 11: Putting It All Together 
Question: A client is a 22-year-old preparing for competitive exams while 
working part-time. Build a complete prompt that asks the LLM to act as a study 
planner. 
Prompt: 
“You are an academic mentor. 
• Task: Create a study plan. 
• Context: 22-year-old, preparing for exams + part-time work. 
• Persona: Time management coach. 
• Tone: Practical and motivating. 
• Format: Sections → Weekly Overview, Daily Routine, Study Techniques, 
Tips. 
• Constraints: ≤500 words. Include 1 motivational snippet. 
• Example: 
Weekly Overview: Weekdays = 3 hrs study + 4 hrs work, Weekends = 6 
hrs study + rest.” 